#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9368b4e-a855-5fab-b074-a3a505c7ab0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f08079f-db0b-5129-b102-96e0d81ffb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f08079f-db0b-5129-b102-96e0d81ffb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247939} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5f6fe9-15a5-57b5-89d0-0367dda26a62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5e9d30f-f89c-59b6-bfdd-32c028f1e2be" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166287" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbbd792-e05f-5d77-acab-60a061084f05" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fe0f18-7bee-5d52-9557-78f4cea4e5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01fe0f18-7bee-5d52-9557-78f4cea4e5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247942} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0534f95-a0f6-52d0-b32f-1882df985c18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65670ee5-4b35-5dbb-8fb1-16eb3b0bfdfc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166288" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/67f6ec4f72e7bd74ac9ae5fa0f195cda1e90e8d8e6fad1bf85944a3dfd17fdfb.png)\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-5$ and $y=5$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }5sin⁡(2x)=DNE.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fec231d-3911-55db-99af-5e3656333afc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change Over a Varying Interval where the Parameter Represents Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f18467-e8c4-5f2a-9490-5aad7a107abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88f18467-e8c4-5f2a-9490-5aad7a107abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247945} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b5322a-db44-505e-987c-b357373625c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fa50f56-e390-5647-861f-69dfd6d9b85e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166289" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhat is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h)-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h))-5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5sin⁡(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2b72a2-c486-5a7a-a65e-57e83b78f6cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Limit at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6ae226-70be-5d67-b026-7fbb33c90afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f6ae226-70be-5d67-b026-7fbb33c90afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247948} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bcc3160-87b1-5fac-bba4-d3eb01e1bea4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a74eea2b-acee-532d-a235-224574818e64" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166290" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhat is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-10.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-10.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-10.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-10.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-10,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59b65fa-d395-5ef3-9a28-1c74301c8104" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6da84be4-441c-5b10-9303-7838438bba58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da84be4-441c-5b10-9303-7838438bba58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247951} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44e53dc-9b7d-5a1d-b726-34376559bed2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f18f67d2-c70c-5699-975d-02f1360b3003" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166291" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhat is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)}=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/e3f5c484211ef0b9cf5f3a29198d25c23933a1c07427d0dcbfad01edbb300d0e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x)$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2072b915-15f9-5296-96dd-707e38df3d10" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With One Quadratic Term by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b72cd2b-0301-5b01-b6e2-0f5449d86af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b72cd2b-0301-5b01-b6e2-0f5449d86af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247954} :answer-field/id #uuid "03797ef6-2c09-5b77-8d9a-266b1d26b8c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247953}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdf224b2-be8d-5605-8767-2ca244ba9954" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166292" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhat is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=\\frac{1-1}{(1)^{2}-3(1)+2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a37ac18-9c6e-5071-8f3a-eef6c5187863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a37ac18-9c6e-5071-8f3a-eef6c5187863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247957} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa9e565-5e47-5c46-ae08-17223af1d3bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247956}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52621623-b8fb-58e2-bbc1-c3b3529706f8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166293" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhat is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\cdot sin⁡(2)$" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}[\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 5sin⁡(2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot sin⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Different Rules to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b218c7e8-4764-5f79-a3df-0d99fa9d3279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{(1+\\pi)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b218c7e8-4764-5f79-a3df-0d99fa9d3279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{(1+\\pi)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247960} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a1f68b-d9e7-58c8-a748-2d5b4ed7805c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247959}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d653b81c-f77c-5d7a-9044-7e51ee37d3bf" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-166294" :question/problem "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhat is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5}{h}\\sin(2h)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-1$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-5$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\cdot sin⁡(2)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}\\frac{2f(x)}{(1+4x)^{3}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}\\frac{2f(x)}{(1+4x)^{3}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}\\frac{2f(x)}{(1+4x)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(1+4x)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n* applying the constant multiple rule in the numerator, and \n\n* the root rule, followed by the sum rule in the denominator, \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(1+4x)^{3}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)}{{({lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(1+4x))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)}{{(1+{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}(4x))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5}{{(1+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\pi )^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
